#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087390 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f249bfa-e57b-5784-9da8-d0f9aff213bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-21813" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = 0$\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = x^4$\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = y$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = 0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this has the form $y' + P(x)y = 0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = x^4.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form $y' + P(x)y = Q(x)$ where $Q(x) = x^4$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{3}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our equation can be written in the form $y' + P(x)y = 0,$ the equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087538 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de671ee-7bba-54ce-bec2-d2d6f5569f4a" :knowledge-point/title "Writing First-Order Linear ODEs in Standard Form"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc93ac2d-3588-5dc4-9cf8-3df1f17c353d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6599" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\ny\\sin x\n-\n\\cos x\n=0\n$$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\sin x$ and $Q(x) = \\cos x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087539 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc50a680-dbf8-5d45-a8c3-72c3c15243f6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying First-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a6d7a3a-38a4-5925-8d1a-3d85b74ad350" :question/math-academy-id "e-6600" :question/problem "Determine whether the following equation is a first-order linear ODE:\n\n$$\nx^2\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\n\\sin(2x)y\n=\ne^x.\n$$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We attempt to write the equation in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x) \\,.\n$$\n\nDividing the equation through by $x^2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)y & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{{x}^{2}}y & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that this equation is now in standard form with $P(x) = \\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{x^2}$ and $Q(x) = \\dfrac{e^x}{x^2}.$\n\nBecause the equation can be written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320866 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc50a680-dbf8-5d45-a8c3-72c3c15243f6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying First-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee48ef50-7901-5968-8c6e-3ce826fd0abf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{3}y=\\frac{x}{1+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320868} {:answer/id #uuid "56a4decd-d69f-5134-a279-4ab7bfe6d830" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+{x}^{2}y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320869} {:answer/id #uuid "b27cb28d-67df-56bb-b535-e57e5c99630d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)=x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320870} {:answer/id #uuid "3858e614-0306-5bff-bc1c-7bc4762692d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320871} {:answer/id #uuid "d0219253-1153-52cd-bc9a-45727be4ddec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy={x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3858e614-0306-5bff-bc1c-7bc4762692d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320871} :answer-field/id #uuid "23eb2ba6-4d1b-5c75-adbb-ad24e70a7072" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "16833ba9-454a-5aa5-8648-06e8c0ad9832" :question/math-academy-id "q-103551" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nis already is written in standard form with  $P(x)=-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc50a680-dbf8-5d45-a8c3-72c3c15243f6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying First-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f225529-def3-54e2-bfe2-a307d38b0b28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295544} {:answer/id #uuid "58bd1127-2f80-596c-9cc9-160f2ff5de85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-{x}^{3}=\\sqrt{1+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295545} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f51840-b743-5f38-a266-57c6a624f8c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{2}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295546} {:answer/id #uuid "de36a199-47ef-5bf5-b198-80d287b3edb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{e}^{xy}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295547} {:answer/id #uuid "fff47300-ef22-525e-a49b-c6e3906d3c77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295548}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f225529-def3-54e2-bfe2-a307d38b0b28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295544} :answer-field/id #uuid "75b6b86d-4093-52ff-a948-ad44ddaf7ebd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a3a7bdfb-b358-530e-a735-49fe9ab8c790" :question/math-academy-id "q-103554" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nis already is written in standard form with  $P(x)=-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}$ and $Q(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087973 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de671ee-7bba-54ce-bec2-d2d6f5569f4a" :knowledge-point/title "Writing First-Order Linear ODEs in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab721a7c-ebb0-5452-b247-5d3816198bf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + (4\\sin x)y = 1 + 3\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084850} {:answer/id #uuid "b319152d-c291-5494-a461-4791daa6c062" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + (4\\cos x)y = 1 + 3\\sin x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084851} {:answer/id #uuid "c17d0099-f48a-51bc-b6bf-feeec39e8bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + (2\\sin x)y = \\frac{1 + 3\\cos x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084852} {:answer/id #uuid "2a507547-4026-5e0f-ae6c-972cd79efc1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{1 + 3\\cos x}{2} = (2\\sin x)y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084853} {:answer/id #uuid "606289fa-36e0-57ab-b7c2-57b9dbbb4540" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + (2\\sin x)y = 1 + \\frac{3}{2}\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c17d0099-f48a-51bc-b6bf-feeec39e8bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + (2\\sin x)y = \\frac{1 + 3\\cos x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084852} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e534d32-a3b5-5b67-a30a-d8fb0702e472" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5c269fd-ead8-50e4-b16e-9d9e9b4a244c" :question/math-academy-id "q-16400" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$2\\frac{dy}{dx} - 3\\cos x = 1 - 4y\\sin x$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The standard form is $y^{\\prime}+P(x)y=Q(x)$. Move the $y$ term to the left and the cosine term to the right: $2y^{\\prime}+4y\\sin x=1+3\\cos x$. Dividing every term by $2$ gives $y^{\\prime}+(2\\sin x)y=\\frac{1+3\\cos x}{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295570 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1219c8ce-b99f-51b9-93cb-8aa2c264a5c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295572} {:answer/id #uuid "af3d3b41-1abc-5add-b1ee-6fb3df674df2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295573} {:answer/id #uuid "d93abfba-138d-5d5c-b1b7-c518978566ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295574} {:answer/id #uuid "5f4fbc36-6177-5379-b397-43d14ad220a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295575} {:answer/id #uuid "e3b9d07b-dd52-5690-9c5a-a6e17e8a5071" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af3d3b41-1abc-5add-b1ee-6fb3df674df2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295573} :answer-field/id #uuid "4580721f-257e-550b-859d-b876e3e3adc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba5d1a07-b366-5225-a5ad-9036dc55130b" :question/math-academy-id "q-331605" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=3x$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=3x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=3x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation has the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{2}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our equation can be written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0,$ the equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295591 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cf227c3-f20d-5d7f-9976-825b249bfda5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295593} {:answer/id #uuid "9f554a0b-7bff-54aa-bab2-e475db2cf532" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295594} {:answer/id #uuid "d5d29575-c29b-56fe-af62-9f3fab6e0818" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295595} {:answer/id #uuid "28dc1cdf-dce1-503b-b046-0ff022affc17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295596} {:answer/id #uuid "b27b7a48-7b2f-5251-afb0-8fef0d46a904" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f554a0b-7bff-54aa-bab2-e475db2cf532" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295594} :answer-field/id #uuid "498d53ab-9a70-5608-b2fc-0ccb36cb644b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2e6adcc-e613-5599-bfa6-8b950fbde93e" :question/math-academy-id "q-331738" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+4xy=2$\n- ${y}^{′}+4xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+4xy=y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4xy=2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=2$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4xy=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+4xy & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+4xy-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(4x-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088362 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc50a680-dbf8-5d45-a8c3-72c3c15243f6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying First-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c34b9817-7572-5e31-83bd-d3b526daebf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{dy}{dx})^{2} + 2xy = 6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087103} {:answer/id #uuid "dcea8c9a-81e6-5544-8145-205e168f69d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + 2xy = 6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087104} {:answer/id #uuid "f322b6fc-44a0-55f7-915b-d14169f9dc4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + 2xy^{2} = 6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087105} {:answer/id #uuid "2828fc07-5e90-5f6d-b4a7-fc668fb5f126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + 2xy = 6x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087106} {:answer/id #uuid "7f1cbc52-aff7-517a-9bd1-88ad12396227" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{dy}{dx} + 2x = 6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2828fc07-5e90-5f6d-b4a7-fc668fb5f126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + 2xy = 6x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087106} :answer-field/id #uuid "dee99775-1c6a-5ae6-afcf-8bb35a100894" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c6eeef64-0029-5193-805e-25131224aaa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-8349" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The equation $y^{\\prime}+2xy=6x^2$ is first-order and linear: $P(x)=2x$ and $Q(x)=6x^2$. The other choices contain $(y^{\\prime})^2$, a second derivative, $y^2$, or the product $yy^{\\prime}$, each of which violates first-order linear form."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de671ee-7bba-54ce-bec2-d2d6f5569f4a" :knowledge-point/title "Writing First-Order Linear ODEs in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0e9f778-66fa-54e7-acc6-001b43aaaaa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{1}{x}y = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087115} {:answer/id #uuid "c28a08c9-ed5f-57c9-a678-ebf32e926c84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\frac{dy}{dx} + y = 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087116} {:answer/id #uuid "f09d6fcb-7508-53dc-9ebf-082a30a5867b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{1}{x}y - 2 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087117} {:answer/id #uuid "1046320f-9c93-59e8-ae7e-8dbe81821495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} =-\\frac{1}{x}y + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087118} {:answer/id #uuid "4bc89d23-a930-5ede-b341-6ac4428f6277" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2x - x\\frac{dy}{dx}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0e9f778-66fa-54e7-acc6-001b43aaaaa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{1}{x}y = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087115} :answer-field/id #uuid "46b999b3-f12d-5d5e-aa21-cfda0ea51796" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9fbd67f9-88e1-5554-8625-84ac1e940645" :question/math-academy-id "q-8359" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x-y}{x}\\,.\n$$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x-y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x}{x}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & +\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{2x}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & +{{\\frac{1}{x}}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y={{2}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x)=2.$"}]}
